{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Re-verified on the live host 2026-09-05 ~22:24 UTC (read-only SSM) — and ONE FIGURE HAS MOVED MATERIALLY\n\nEverything in this ticket's description still holds, with one exception that changes the urgency.\n\n```\nlsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINT,SERIAL\n  nvme1n1        50G xfs    /var/gerrit   vol06fa2e77a9dd97527\n  nvme0n1        60G                      vol0270fcf13709cf472\n  ├─nvme0n1p1    60G xfs    /\n  └─nvme0n1p128  10M vfat   /boot/efi\n  nvme2n1        50G   ---     ---        vol06780b8557d1416b7   <- still raw, still unmounted\n\nblkid /dev/nvme2n1                       -> NO FILESYSTEM\n/etc/fstab                               -> /, /boot/efi, /var/gerrit ONLY. No gate-scratch line.\nmountpoint /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch   -> is not a mountpoint\n.gate-scratch-required                   -> No such file or directory\n.gate-scratch-mounted                    -> No such file or directory\n```\n\nLive contents, mtimes at the moment of inspection (22:21-22:24), i.e. **being written right now**:\n`rebar-gate-snapshots` (203 entries), `reviewbot-r7x216ij`, `semgrep-mcp`, and five loose\n`tmp*` files.\n\n**THE CHANGE — root has gone from 57% to 81%.**\n\n```\n                 ticket (earlier)      now\n  df -h /        34G / 60G  (57%)      49G / 60G  (81%)   12G free\n  df -h /var/gerrit                    11G / 50G  (21%)\n```\n\nRoot has gained roughly **15G in about eleven hours** while gate scratch runs on it. The ticket's\n\"stable, which is why this is not a p1\" assessment was true when written and is weaker now: at this\nrate the margin is days, not weeks, and a full root takes Gerrit down with it — the exact coupling\nS1 removed on paper. I am not re-prioritising the ticket unilaterally, but an operator deciding\nwhen to take the quiet window should weigh 12G free, not 26G.\n\n## Two findings that change the remediation sequence\n\n**1. A `du` over this directory does not complete in a usable time.** I started a read-only\n`du -sh --one-file-system` on the scratch tree at 22:30 UTC; it had not returned after **25\nminutes**, so I cancelled it rather than add load to a host with documented IOPS saturation. So I\ndid NOT re-measure the 9.3G figure, and I do not trust it as current given root grew 15G. The\npractical consequence: **the remediation must not depend on `du`** — every space check should use\n`df`, which is O(1). The runbook I am writing does exactly that.\n\n**2. The review-bot container binds the PARENT, so a host mount will not reach it.**\n`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:305` binds `/var/lib/rebar:/var/lib/rebar`, not the mount point\n(deliberately — the comment there explains that binding only the mount point would make the\ndeclaration marker invisible and the guard unable to fire). Docker bind mounts default to\n`rprivate` propagation, so **a mount created on the host UNDERNEATH that existing bind does not\nappear inside the already-running container.**\n\nThat means an operator who quiesces, mounts, writes the markers and then merely `docker compose\nstart review-bot` gets a split brain: the host shows a correctly mounted, empty volume and every\nhost-side check says \"fixed\", while the bot keeps writing to the OLD root directory through its\npre-existing bind. **`docker compose up -d --force-recreate review-bot` is mandatory, not tidiness.**\nThis is not stated anywhere in the ticket or in `review-bot-ops.md`, and it is the single most\nlikely way this remediation gets declared done while remaining in effect.\n\nNothing was mutated: read-only inspection only, and my one long-running probe was cancelled."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788648429504775001,"uuid":"8bcacad1-d716-44c1-8e2f-5c00f45448a8"}